  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Programme Co-ordinator&apos;s salary support - UHAI HIV and  AIDS Prevention, Care and Support Programme</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Arusha Archdiocesan Integrated Development and Relief Office (AAIDRO) 2000 is the Development office of the Catholic Archdiocese of Arusha. AAIDRO, which is entrusted with empowering the marginalized and promoting sustainable development among the communities in the Archdiocese, operates in seven districts of Arusha and Manyara regions namely Arumeru, Arusha, Longido, Monduli, Ngorongoro, Simanjiro and Kiteto.
HIV and AIDS is one of the projects managed by AAIDRO. HIV and AIDS Prevention and Training project under appraisal has provided HIV and AIDS services to community members for about 20 years. Services provided under this project include VCT services and Home Based Care, HIV Prevention and treatment, strengthening and support of clubs PLHIV and OVCs, support to most vulnerable children (MVC), nutritional support to PLHIV and skills training for PLHIV including support to initiate income generation activities.
HIV and AIDS Prevention project evaluation findings have evidence that the project changed lives of beneficiaries positively. This evidenced by testimonials from beneficiary groups of PLHIV, MVC and the wider community including Local Government leaders.  The target groups for the project under implementation are people living with HIV and AIDS and their families, orphans and most vulnerable children, widows and volunteers. The number of direct beneficiaries is estimated at 1,500 while the indirect beneficiaries (community members) are estimated at 17,000. 
During the 2010-14 implementation period, AAIDRO opted for a Programmatic Approach; a move supported by AAIDRO staff and partners. The proposal to CAFOD was informed by discussions around the approach which involved harmonization of Archdiocesan HIV and AIDS related projects for a holistic comprehensive response and in order to improve service delivery and utilization of resources.  Following the refocusing exercise (January &#x2013; March 2012), CAFOD recommended a responsible programme closure in Tanzania.  During the financial period 2013/14 CAFOD specifically supported Archdiocese of Arusha to roll out exit strategies that aimed at ensuring continuity of project activities after CAFOD&#x2019;s phase out from Tanzania.
This grant has been approved to facilitate the Arch Bishop&#x2019;s request to CAFOD for financial support to specifically support the Programme Coordinator&#x2019;s salary amounting to &#xa3;6,500 for one year. The Project Coordinator will continue to strengthen the integration of HIV &amp; AIDS within the Health department of Uhai Centre with 12 health facilities. This request is in compliance with the recommended CAFOD&#x2019;s &#x201c;responsive exit&#x201d; and the newly created Diocesan structures to ensure programme sustainability. It is anticipated that this grant will enable the Arch Diocese achieve continuity of CAFOD work beyond the programme closure by strengthening HIV activities in health structures over the coming year</narrative>
  </description>
